1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,216
This video is about aliasing and mutation. Aliasing means two names for one object. Mutation means a change to an object.

2
00:00:09,666 --> 00:00:21,549
We use the object diagrams from the last video. We solve two past exam questions on lists: one mutable, one immutable. At the end we see the keyword final.

3
00:00:21,999 --> 00:00:28,015
The course notes define aliasing: multiple variables all refer to the same object.

4
00:00:28,465 --> 00:00:34,886
In Superman's story, at line 35, Clark Kent and Superman both point to Person 3.

5
00:00:35,336 --> 00:00:47,795
The notes say that three names, Kal El, Clark Kent and Superman, refer to Person 3. Be exact about time. Kal El and Clark Kent were aliases after line 28.

6
00:00:48,245 --> 00:00:55,605
Line 31 set Kal El to null. So at line 35 the aliases are Clark Kent and Superman.

7
00:00:56,055 --> 00:01:01,964
Aliasing can make fiction more colourful, but it makes programs harder to read and understand.

8
00:01:02,414 --> 00:01:09,155
The notes make the point with Dostoevsky's novel, The Brothers Karamazov. Each brother has many names.

9
00:01:09,605 --> 00:01:13,360
Line 5 makes Brother 1, and Dmitri points to it.

10
00:01:13,810 --> 00:01:20,018
Lines 6 to 9 copy the arrow of Dmitri four times. Five names, one object.

11
00:01:20,468 --> 00:01:27,999
Lines 11 to 14 do the same for the second brother. Ivan and three more names point to Brother 2.

12
00:01:28,449 --> 00:01:32,715
Lines 16 to 23 give the third brother eight names.

13
00:01:33,165 --> 00:01:45,923
This time each line copies the previous name, not Alexei. Look at line 18: Alyoshka equals Alyosha. The right side is Alyosha. Follow its arrow to Brother 3.

14
00:01:46,373 --> 00:01:54,373
So Alyoshka points to Brother 3, not to the slot Alyosha. A chain of copies still ends at one object.

15
00:01:54,823 --> 00:01:57,297
Three new statements make three objects.

16
00:01:57,747 --> 00:02:01,033
Five plus four plus eight is seventeen names.

17
00:02:01,483 --> 00:02:05,643
This is the object diagram at P C 24 in the notes.

18
00:02:06,093 --> 00:02:22,327
The Brothers Karamazov was made to have many aliases. The next story, Where The Wild Things Are, is a more realistic program. Here aliasing comes from procedure calls, and some aliases make the program easier to understand.

19
00:02:22,777 --> 00:02:29,241
Two aliases are added to aid understanding. Line 5: Max In Wolf Suit is Max.

20
00:02:29,691 --> 00:02:31,718
Line 19: King is p.

21
00:02:32,168 --> 00:02:41,619
Line 12: the parameter p. A procedure parameter is an alias that you cannot avoid. Every call copies an arrow into p.

22
00:02:42,069 --> 00:02:47,338
Two lines mutate a field. Line 13 sets p dot isLonely to true.

23
00:02:47,788 --> 00:02:57,260
Line 10 sets Max dot isLonely to false. The comment on line 13 asks: who is p? We run the program to find out.

24
00:02:57,710 --> 00:03:04,857
Lines 3 and 4. Mother is Person 1, and Max is Person 2. Both start with isLonely false.

25
00:03:05,307 --> 00:03:09,360
Line 5: Max In Wolf Suit copies the arrow of Max.

26
00:03:09,810 --> 00:03:16,786
Line 8 calls sail Away with Max In Wolf Suit. The new frame has slots p, King and wildthings.

27
00:03:17,236 --> 00:03:21,311
p gets the same arrow as Max In Wolf Suit, to Person 2.

28
00:03:21,761 --> 00:03:27,201
Line 13: p dot isLonely equals true. Follow p to Person 2.

29
00:03:27,651 --> 00:03:35,928
isLonely of Person 2 is now true. So line 13 changes the object of Max, although it never says Max.

30
00:03:36,378 --> 00:03:46,511
Lines 14 and 15 make an array and three Wild Things. The slot wildthings points to the array, and the array points to the three objects.

31
00:03:46,961 --> 00:03:57,756
Line 18 tests p dot can Stare Without Blinking. Line 4 made Max with true for this field. So the test is true, and line 19 runs.

32
00:03:58,206 --> 00:04:05,993
Line 19: King equals p. Now Max, Max In Wolf Suit, p and King are four names for Person 2.

33
00:04:06,443 --> 00:04:13,099
Line 25 tests King dot isLonely. Follow King to Person 2: isLonely is true.

34
00:04:13,549 --> 00:04:20,589
So the program reaches line 32. This is the object diagram at P C 32 in the notes.

35
00:04:21,039 --> 00:04:26,692
Mutation means a change to an object. Line 13 mutates p dot isLonely.

36
00:04:27,142 --> 00:04:30,022
Line 10 mutates Max dot isLonely.

37
00:04:30,472 --> 00:04:38,173
The reader of the story knows that both lines change the same object, Person 2. p and Max are aliases.

38
00:04:38,623 --> 00:04:51,274
This is hard for the compiler to know. It must do an inter procedural pointer analysis. That is our object diagram, but built for every program point and every possible input.

39
00:04:51,724 --> 00:05:00,577
It is even harder for a programmer in a large program that is not a famous story. Be careful about mutation and aliasing.

40
00:05:01,027 --> 00:05:18,841
Now a past exam question, from the slides and questions pre 2.1.1. The program uses a linked list from the J D K, the Java Development Kit. The J D K lists are mutable. What does line 10 print? Commit to one answer.

41
00:05:19,291 --> 00:05:20,933
Pause the video and try it.

42
00:05:21,383 --> 00:05:26,781
Run it by hand. Line 6: new makes Linked List 1, and list points to it.

43
00:05:27,231 --> 00:05:34,676
Line 7: alias equals list. No new, so no new bubble. alias points to the same Linked List 1.

44
00:05:35,126 --> 00:05:42,550
Line 8: list dot add hello. Follow list to Linked List 1. The add method mutates this object.

45
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:52,408
add makes a node inside the list, and the node refers to the string hello. The new for the node is inside the library code of add.

46
00:05:52,858 --> 00:05:59,279
Line 9: alias dot add world. alias leads to the same object, Linked List 1.

47
00:05:59,729 --> 00:06:03,079
So the same list gets a second node, with world.

48
00:06:03,529 --> 00:06:06,515
Line 10 prints the list: hello, world.

49
00:06:06,965 --> 00:06:14,432
The answer is c. Lines 8 and 9 both mutate one object, because list and alias are aliases.

50
00:06:14,882 --> 00:06:21,218
Mutable means can be changed. The J D K lists are mutable: add changes the list.

51
00:06:21,668 --> 00:06:31,396
Immutable means cannot be changed. Parboiled is the parser generator that the labs use later. It comes with a class called Immutable List.

52
00:06:31,846 --> 00:06:34,129
Start with an empty Immutable List.

53
00:06:34,579 --> 00:06:43,304
To add an item, you call append. Append does not change the list. It makes a new list with the old contents and the new item.

54
00:06:43,754 --> 00:06:45,503
The old list stays empty.

55
00:06:45,953 --> 00:06:55,041
Compiler engineers want this for a reason. Procedure calls make aliases that you cannot avoid, and a compiler has many calls.

56
00:06:55,491 --> 00:07:06,201
Immutable data makes the code easier to reason about, gives fewer bugs, and makes debugging easier. The trade off is that adding an item feels different.

57
00:07:06,651 --> 00:07:23,803
The second exam question, from the slides and questions pre 2.1.2. The same program shape, but with Parboiled's Immutable List and append. What does line 9 print? And what does line 12 print? Each has the same four options.

58
00:07:24,253 --> 00:07:25,895
Pause the video and try it.

59
00:07:26,345 --> 00:07:33,193
Line 5: Immutable List dot of makes an empty list, Immutable List 1. list points to it.

60
00:07:33,643 --> 00:07:36,907
Line 6: alias copies the arrow of list.

61
00:07:37,357 --> 00:07:44,184
Line 7: list dot append hello. Append makes a new list, Immutable List 2, with hello.

62
00:07:44,634 --> 00:07:53,807
But the program does not assign the result. No slot points to Immutable List 2, so we draw it gray. List 1 does not change.

63
00:07:54,257 --> 00:08:00,593
Line 8 does the same with alias. It makes Immutable List 3 with world, and throws it away.

64
00:08:01,043 --> 00:08:06,569
Line 9 prints list. list still points to Immutable List 1, which is empty.

65
00:08:07,019 --> 00:08:11,264
The output is the empty list. The answer for line 9 is a.

66
00:08:11,714 --> 00:08:25,111
Line 10: list equals list dot append hello. The right side first: list is Immutable List 1. Append makes Immutable List 4 with hello. It refers to the same String 1.

67
00:08:25,561 --> 00:08:32,409
This time we assign the result. Erase the old arrow of list, and draw it to Immutable List 4.

68
00:08:32,859 --> 00:08:43,014
Line 11: alias equals alias dot append world. Resolve alias first. It still points to Immutable List 1, the empty list.

69
00:08:43,464 --> 00:08:47,027
So append makes Immutable List 5 with only world.

70
00:08:47,477 --> 00:08:53,663
alias now points to Immutable List 5. list and alias are no longer aliases.

71
00:08:54,113 --> 00:08:56,481
Line 12 prints list: hello.

72
00:08:56,931 --> 00:09:10,051
The answer for line 12 is b, not hello world. Each name got its own new list. Nothing was shared, so nothing was mutated behind our back. We checked both outputs in Java.

73
00:09:10,501 --> 00:09:16,923
The notes also show the right way. Line 5: list points to the empty Immutable List 1.

74
00:09:17,373 --> 00:09:25,415
Line 6: list equals list dot append hello. The new Immutable List 2 holds hello, and list moves to it.

75
00:09:25,865 --> 00:09:34,719
Line 7: append world to Immutable List 2. The new Immutable List 3 holds hello and world, and list moves to it.

76
00:09:35,169 --> 00:09:37,387
Line 8 prints hello, world.

77
00:09:37,837 --> 00:09:44,920
With an immutable list, always assign the result of append. The old lists stay unchanged in the heap.

78
00:09:45,370 --> 00:09:53,754
Last topic: the keyword final. In Java, final prevents re-assignment. Line 4 declares a final string s.

79
00:09:54,204 --> 00:10:01,649
When you write final, the compiler must prove that the variable is assigned exactly once on every program path.

80
00:10:02,099 --> 00:10:06,430
A procedure parameter can also be final, as on line 3.

81
00:10:06,880 --> 00:10:11,189
msg is declared final on line 7, without a value.

82
00:10:11,639 --> 00:10:14,669
If args is empty, line 8 assigns it.

83
00:10:15,119 --> 00:10:17,295
Otherwise, line 9 assigns it.

84
00:10:17,745 --> 00:10:25,275
Both paths meet at line 10. On each path msg was assigned exactly once. So javac accepts it.

85
00:10:25,725 --> 00:10:39,784
The field f of class C is final too: the compiler proves that every constructor assigns it exactly once. After the midterm, we learn how a compiler makes this proof, with dataflow analysis.

86
00:10:40,234 --> 00:10:46,591
Here is the run with no command line arguments. Line 4: s points to the string known.

87
00:10:47,041 --> 00:10:52,609
args dot length is 0, so line 8 runs: msg points to no args.

88
00:10:53,059 --> 00:11:00,739
Line 10 makes C 1. The constructor sets its final field f to the same string. c points to C 1.

89
00:11:01,189 --> 00:11:06,864
The notes also draw the literal some args. No arrow points to it, so we draw it gray.

90
00:11:07,314 --> 00:11:11,730
This is the object diagram at P C 11, as in the notes.

91
00:11:12,180 --> 00:11:20,927
Line 11 prints c dot f: no args. Every slot here was set in exactly one place, and never changed after.

92
00:11:21,377 --> 00:11:28,886
Now a wrong use, from the notes. Line 3 sets the final s, and line 4 tries to assign s again.

93
00:11:29,336 --> 00:11:36,952
javac refuses: cannot assign a value to final variable s. A final slot keeps its first arrow.

94
00:11:37,402 --> 00:11:43,653
Compare the two ideas. final is about a slot: its arrow is set once and never moves.

95
00:11:44,103 --> 00:11:48,284
Immutable is about an object: its contents never change.

96
00:11:48,734 --> 00:11:56,926
So final alone does not stop mutation. Our example: a final list variable, and then list dot add hello.

97
00:11:57,376 --> 00:12:04,459
It compiles, and it prints hello. The arrow out of the slot list did not move, but the object changed.

98
00:12:04,909 --> 00:12:17,858
The notes combine the two. With an immutable data structure and final, a value is set in exactly one place and does not change after that. This makes the program easier to reason about.

99
00:12:18,308 --> 00:12:27,631
One more note from the course notes: Java final is similar to, but not exactly the same as, the C and C plus plus keyword const.

100
00:12:28,081 --> 00:12:36,422
To summarize. Aliasing means several names point to one object. A copy follows the arrow, and never points to a slot.

101
00:12:36,872 --> 00:12:44,851
Procedure parameters are aliases that you cannot avoid. In the Wild Things story, p and Max are one object.

102
00:12:45,301 --> 00:12:53,215
A mutation through one alias is seen through all of them. That is why the linked list program prints hello, world.

103
00:12:53,665 --> 00:13:02,945
An immutable append makes a new list. Assign the result, or it is lost. The Parboiled program prints the empty list, then hello.

104
00:13:03,395 --> 00:13:16,601
final means assigned exactly once on every path, and the compiler proves it. It fixes the slot, not the object. Practice these diagrams. Object diagrams are midterm material.
